To melt, ions cannot be in fixed locations. Because each ion in these
compounds forms strong bonds to neighboring ions, considerable energy
is required to free them. Still more energy is needed to move ions out of
the liquid state and cause boiling.

As a result of their high boiling points, ionic compounds are rarely
gaseous at room temperature, while many molecular compounds are. Ice,
for example, will eventually melt and then vaporize. In contrast, salt will
remain a solid no matter how long it remains at room temperature.

