Determining Molecular Shapes
Lewis structures are two-dimensional and do not show the three-
dimensional shape of a molecule. However, the three-dimensional shape
of a molecule is important in determining the molecule’s physical and
chemical properties. Sugar, or sucrose, is an example. Sucrose has a shape
that fits certain nerve receptors on the tongue. Once stimulated, the
nerves send signals to the brain, and the brain interprets these signals as
sweetness. Inside body cells, sucrose is processed for energy.

People who want to avoid sucrose in their diet often use a sugar sub-
stitute, such as sucralose, shown in Figure 14. These substitutes have
shapes similar to that of sucrose, so they can stimulate the nerve receptors
in the same way that sucrose does. However, sucralose has a different
chemical makeup than sucrose does and cannot be processed by the body.

