aqueous solutions contain iodide and lead(II) ions, which would form
lead(II) iodide. So, the net change is the same.

Net ionic equations can also be used to describe displacement reac-
tions. For example, Zn reacts with a solution of CuSO4 and displaces the
copper ion, Cu2+, as shown in the total ionic equation

Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) + SO2−
4 (aq) → Cu(s) + Zn2+(aq) + SO2−

4 (aq)

Only the sulfate ion remains unchanged and is a spectator ion. Thus, the
net ionic equation is as follows:

Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) → Cu(s) + Zn2+(aq)

