Heat Results in Disorderly Particle Motion
When a substance receives energy in the form of heat, its enthalpy
increases and the kinetic energy of the particles that make up the sub-
stance increases. The direction in which any particle moves is not related
to the direction in which its neighboring particles move. The motions of
these particles are random.

Suppose the substance was a rubber ball and you kicked the ball
across a field. The energy that you gave the ball produces a different
result than heat because the energy caused the particles in the ball to
move together and in the same direction.The kinetic energy that you gave
the particles in the ball is not random but is concerted.

Do you notice any relationships between energy and motion? Heat
often produces disorderly particle motion. Other types of energy can
produce orderly motion or orderly positioning of particles.

