Particles attract each other, so it takes energy to overcome the forces
holding them together. If it takes high energy to separate the particles of
a substance, then it takes high energy to cause that substance to go from
the liquid to the gaseous state. The boiling point of a substance is a good
measure of the strength of the forces that hold the particles together.
Melting point also relates to attractive forces between particles. Most
covalent compounds melt at lower temperatures than ionic compounds
do. As shown in Table 1, ionic substances with small ions tend to be solids
that have high melting points, and covalent substances tend to be gases
and liquids or solids that have low melting points.

