Calculating the Voltage of a Cell
Think of E° as a measure of the ability of an electrode to gain electrons.
A more positive value means the electrode is more likely to be a cathode.
The standard cell voltage—the voltage of a cell under standard condi-
tions—can be found by subtracting the standard potentials of the two
electrodes, as follows:

E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode

To determine the reaction that will happen naturally, use the electrode with
the most positive E° value as the cathode, as shown in Sample Problem C.
Otherwise, a given reaction happens naturally if E°cell is positive. If E°cell is
negative, the reaction could be made to happen if energy is added.

