to one another by a single bond and arranged in a linear fashion. Notice,
however, that each molecule, with the exception of butane, has a different
functional group attached to one or more of these carbon atoms. As a
result, each molecule has properties that differ greatly from butane.

For example, compare the boiling point of butane with those of the
other compounds in Table 2. Butane is a gas at room temperature. Because
of the symmetrical arrangement of the atoms, butane is nonpolar. Because
the intermolecular forces between butane molecules are weak, butane has
very low boiling and melting points and a lower density than the other
four-carbon molecules.

