Hydrogenation Is a Common Addition Reaction
A common type of addition reaction is hydrogenation, in which one or
more hydrogen atoms are added to an unsaturated molecule. As a result
of hydrogenation, the product of the reaction contains fewer double or
triple bonds than the reactant. Hydrogenation is used to convert veg-
etable oils into fats. Vegetable oils are long chains of carbon atoms with
many double bonds. When hydrogen gas is bubbled through an oil, dou-
ble bonds between carbon atoms in the oil are broken and hydrogen
