The Two Stages of Cellular Respiration SciLinks code: HW4018

Cellular respiration has two stages. Both stages produce ATP. The first
stage of cellular respiration includes glycolysis.The name means “glucose-
splitting,” which makes sense because the six-carbon glucose is split into
two molecules of pyruvic acid, CH3COCOOH or C3H4O3. The glycolysis
reaction has about a dozen steps. Other products react further to make
more ATP. The net gain of eight ATP is shown in the following equation.

C6H12O6 + O2 + 8ADP + 8H2PO−
4 → 2C3H4O3 + 8ATP + 10H2O

The second stage of cellular respiration, called the Kreb’s cycle, also
has several steps. The overall result is the oxidation of pyruvic acid to
form CO2, as shown in the following equation.

2C3H4O3 + 5O2 + 30ADP + 30H2PO−
4 → 6CO2 + 30ATP + 34H2O

The two stages together produce 38 ATP ions per glucose molecule.
The reaction for glucose has an enthalpy change of −1273 kJ. Thus,
(38 × −20 kJ)/(−1273 kJ) or 60% of the energy of glucose has been stored
as ATP. The remaining energy helps to keep the body warm.

